{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792307731,"nonce":"eRXZ8fOeOhm19WdLgLTpf+kc6jE=","title":"OSR: an inner loop guarded against running zero times is never a resume point","message":"A `for i in range(j + 1, n)` nested in another loop lowers to a guard that skips the inner loop when its range is empty, then the loop. A value the outer pass defines before the inner loop and reads after it (the outer counter, say) reaches the read both from the inner loop's exit and from the skip edge. `osr_layout_with` repaired such a live-in with a phi at the resumed header alone, and refused the layout (`RegionHasExternalEntry`) whenever a read sat at a join the definition reaches around the header. Every inner loop of that shape went without a site, so an interpreted frame could leave only at the outermost loop's header.\n\nscimark LU (Python, `--benchmark=LU 100 200`, one iteration as CI runs it): the first `LU_factor` call interprets until 16 visits of its outer `j` loop, which is about 40% of the factorization. That call took 218 ms, every later call about 0.9 ms; the iteration printed 0.42 s on the Cranelift build and 0.37 s with LLVM, against PyPy's 0.215 s. With `ZYNTAX_BASELINE_THRESHOLD=1` the function compiles before its first call and the iteration was 0.17 s, which is how the gap showed up.\n\nFix: the repair places phis at the definition's iterated dominance frontier within the region entered at the header, and renames each block's reads to the definition reaching it. Probes in compiled code keep refusing layouts that read through such a join (`osr_probe_layout_with`): a probe at an inner header holds every repaired live-in on every pass, which cost a quarter of LU's steady-state call time when tried. `promote_static_hot` uses the same rule, since it asks at loops the baseline frame passes through.\n\nAfter: the first call is about 28 ms; the iteration 0.22 s on Cranelift.","files":null}]}